<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Common Product Resource Planning Challenges and How to Address Them</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Organizations often face resource constraints when multiple product
                    initiatives compete for the same people, skills, budget, or technical
                    capabilities. Identifying these challenges helps teams create more
                    reliable and efficient resource plans.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Limited Resource Availability</b></h5>
                <p>
                    Limited availability of skilled employees can delay important product
                    initiatives. Organizations can address this by prioritizing critical
                    work, balancing workloads, and identifying capability gaps early.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Competing Product Priorities</b></h5>
                <p>
                    When several initiatives require the same resources, teams can become
                    overloaded. A clear prioritization framework helps determine where
                    resources should be allocated first.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Skill Gaps</b></h5>
                <p>
                    Product initiatives may require specialized technical or business
                    expertise that is not currently available. Identifying skill gaps
                    early allows organizations to plan training, hiring, outsourcing, or
                    other capability-building options.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Unbalanced Workloads</b></h5>
                <p>
                    Uneven workload distribution can affect productivity and delivery
                    quality. Regular capacity reviews help managers identify overloaded
                    teams and redistribute responsibilities where appropriate.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Unexpected Changes</b></h5>
                <p>
                    Changes in customer requirements, business priorities, or product
                    strategy can quickly change resource requirements. Flexible planning
                    allows teams to adapt without unnecessarily disrupting delivery.
                </p>

                <h5><b>Insufficient Resource Visibility</b></h5>
                <p>
                    Without a centralized view of capacity, skills, workload, and upcoming
                    requirements, resource decisions can become reactive. Transparent
                    resource planning improves decision-making and coordination.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add the relevant resource planning image here when available. *@
            </div>
